 begins in the past and  projects into the future and I think  it's nothing sadder than being the very  last member of your tribe like the last  person to speak a dying language the  other thing it does for us is it gives  us a kind of recognition we all crave to  be respected but it matters who's  offering the respect and the people who  count are the people whose tribe we're  part of so as you can see I'm an  academic and what matters to me is the  respect I get from other academics and  bus drivers probably think what I do is  a complete waste of time maybe right it  doesn't bother me that doesn't bother me  so now why isn't it enough just to be  respected as a human being and I think  that's too emptier description what  about for your individual achievements  well that's fine if you're Usain Bolt or  edge here and but I think for most of us  there's nothing very special that we've  achieved so what matters is that we're  seen as being a good enough member of  the tribe that we belong to so tribes do  good things for their own members but  don't they create problems for outsiders  what about inter tribal conflicts well I  think essentially these arise when one  tribe poses a threat to the well-being  or the very existence of another so  classically tribe a invades the hunting  grounds of tribe B so the important  thing is to give tribes a secure space  to operate in and the big problem occurs  when a whole society divides along  tribal lines whether these are ethnic or  religious or political because then  physical separation becomes impossible  that's what we want to avoid at all  costs and the way to do it is to have an  inclusive identity so that people who  have unavoidably to live together can  all see themselves as belonging to  one big tribe and that's why I'm happy  to call myself a liberal nationalist  because I see nation-building as the  constructive way to the to respond to  the human need to belong that I'm saying  is inescapable I'm not even so sure that  we should describe the conflicts we have  today as tribal first of all I think the  word tribal is a particular  anthropological understanding of an  ecology of people living together in  different times than the ones we do and  I just got to say that you know is this  translation is this metaphor the right  one now do we are we all really proud of  our families that we already proud of  the nations we belong to a simplistic we  love our parents we hate our parents our  parents [ __ ] us up what did Philip Locke  advanced right a parent sometimes [ __ ]  ourselves then we recover our nations we  love we we fight of the the patriotism  of our nation because we think it's  misguided so I think that there are a  whole complex set of feelings even in  the most intimate institutions of our  lives and that doesn't fit well with  this narrow notion of you know you love  where you came from many of us come from  many different places in our lives and  each time we embed or see the certain  kind of home and I think that we've got  to think about that in the world in  which we live now I also think that this  idea of universal humanity doesn't  really work  after all it's a very important issue  that we have in the Universal  Declaration of Human Rights but very  often we think of universal humanity  after we have persecuted people after we  persecute people then we could set up  these you know even in the Enlightenment  you know after the 30-year war you began  to get the issue of actually  constituting nations and this is my  point  nations are our tribes  and the good and the evil come together  because we our nations are our tribes  how we constitute within a nation a set  of convergent beliefs practices a sense  of a sense of common purpose that's very  important but at the moment we are  seeing tribal nationalism and tribal  nationalism it is a term I get from  Hannah Arendt writing in a period very  near our like our own in the interwar  period where you had mass migrations and  nation-states were either forming or  being deconstructed and what she says  about tribal nationalism I think is  something we have to urgently hear today  she says tribal nationalists or tribal  national moments are constituted often  by large masculinist fingers with 64 in  chests who believe and this is the  description that mr modi gives of  himself I have a 64 in chest I will take  all the beatings for my people I have a  broad chest mr. Trump has his own before  by which he thought of you know holds up  no policy no argument but he does have  this rather impressive before and he  tries to live by so the whole issue  really here is how do we how do we  defeat this nationalist issue and I just  want to say that the best way to do this  is to make ourselves aware of what the  symptoms of it are whatever the economic  discontents whatever the failures of  globalization whatever the failures of  the EU why is it that in every country  every time the issue always comes up as  my immigration racism ethnic policy  whatever the economic issues are it is  unquestionable that african-americans in  America do worse than white that then  then the oppressed and in a way  suffering white populations there's no  question about that make the good-doers  but somehow as soon as we have within  the nation-state form  some forms of nationalism it's always  race gender sexuality femininity that  begins to become the enemy of the people  and I think we've got to be very clear  about that as a symptom it's not the  cause it's the symptom it's not the  cause of reason it's not the cause of  the wall being built between Mexico in  the United States I want to end actually  with a voice much finer than mine a poem  about I believe a short term about  populist nationalism or tribal  nationalism by WH Auden it's called  diaspora how he survived them they could  never understand how the victim somehow  survives the oppressor that's what  drives the tyrant man how he survived  them they could never understand had  they not beggared him themselves to  prove they could not live without their  dogmas or their land no worlds they  drove him from wherever big enough how  could it be the earth the unconfined  meant when it bade them to set no limits  to their love on heat with fear he drew  their terrorists to him and was a  godsend to the lowest of mankind till  there was no place left where they could  still pursue him except that exile which  they called his race but envying him  even that they plunge right through him  into a land of mirrors without time for  space and all they had to strike now was  the human face  [Applause]
 

